From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:38:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wpa_supplicant: Skip the OBSS check of an IBSS or mesh with fixed_freq

On 5 GHz and 6 GHz, ibss_mesh_select_40mhz() checks the scan results
for other BSSs on the primary and secondary channels of a 40 MHz
channel. check_40mhz_5g() can then swap the primary and secondary
channels, and a failure to get the scan results falls back to 20 MHz.
This also happens with fixed_freq=1, so an IBSS or mesh with a fixed
frequency can start on another primary channel. Mesh STAs with
different scan results can then select different primary channels.

Skip the check with fixed_freq=1, for IBSS and mesh. Document
fixed_freq in wpa_supplicant.conf, which did not describe it.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
---

--- a/wpa_supplicant/config_ssid.h
+++ b/wpa_supplicant/config_ssid.h
@@ -565,7 +565,7 @@ struct wpa_ssid {
 	u8 edmg_channel;
 
 	/**
-	 * fixed_freq - Use fixed frequency for IBSS
+	 * fixed_freq - Use fixed frequency for IBSS or mesh
 	 */
 	int fixed_freq;
 
--- a/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant.c
+++ b/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant.c
@@ -3592,6 +3592,9 @@ void ibss_mesh_setup_freq(struct wpa_sup
 
 	freq->freq = ssid->frequency;
 
+	if (ssid->fixed_freq)
+		obss_scan = 0;
+
 	if (ssid->mode == WPAS_MODE_IBSS && !ssid->fixed_freq) {
 		struct wpa_bss *bss = ibss_find_existing_bss(wpa_s, ssid);
 
--- a/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant.conf
+++ b/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant.conf
@@ -1021,6 +1021,16 @@ fast_reauth=1
 # an IBSS network with the configured SSID is already present, the frequency of
 # the network will be used instead of this configured value.
 #
+# fixed_freq: Whether to use the configured frequency as is (IBSS and mesh)
+# 0 = adapt the channel to the other networks found in the scan results
+#     (default)
+# 1 = use the configured frequency as the primary channel
+# With fixed_freq=1, an IBSS does not move to the frequency of an IBSS with the
+# same SSID, and the driver is asked to keep the frequency. On 5 GHz and 6 GHz,
+# an IBSS or mesh also does not check the other BSSs on the channels of a
+# 40 MHz channel. That check can move the primary channel to the secondary
+# channel or fall back to 20 MHz.
+#
 # pbss: Whether to use PBSS. Relevant to IEEE 802.11ad networks only.
 # 0 = do not use PBSS
 # 1 = use PBSS
